                  },
                  "text": "<div>Enteric coatings are placed on the medications mostly if they are irritating to the stomach or they need to dissolve in the small intestine so that they are protected from the stomach acids.</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Enteric coated medications must never be chewed, broken in half, or crushed.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, the enteric coating dissolves in the small intestine and does not delay the half life or extend the drug's effect in any way. The sustained release medications will dissolve over a longer period of time.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Well done,&nbsp;the enteric coating dissolves in the small intestine and does not delay the half life or extend the drug's effect in any way. The sustained release medications will dissolve over a longer period of time.</div>\n",
                    "tip": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Enteric coatings are placed on the medication to make their effects last longer.&nbsp;</div>\n"
